<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # This Is a Local Artist. – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 67%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Arts and Crafts</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> This Is a Local Artist.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Analyze simple editorials or opinion pieces. Reading: Create visual presentations summarizing informational texts. Phonemic Awareness: Demonstrate knowledge of phonemic awareness by applying knowledge of affixes to base words in context.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can analyze simple editorials or opinion pieces related to arts and crafts, identifying key themes and viewpoints (e.g., "What is the cultural significance of this piece of art").
                Can create a drawing or a model of a famous historical building based on a text description (e.g., "The Biomuseo is a colorful building with a unique design by Frank Gehry").
                Can decode and accurately pronounce words by applying knowledge of affixes to base words in context, such as past tense verbs and gerunds, enhancing phonemic awareness (e.g., played, playing).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification of prior knowledge and target vocabulary.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a collage of images featuring local Panamanian art and crafts, such as molas, tagua carvings, traditional baskets, and the Biomuseo building. Students work in pairs to identify the crafts and artists they discussed in the previous lesson (Lesson 1 vocabulary recall). The teacher encourages them to use adjectives like 'colorful,' 'unique,' 'handmade,' and verbs like 'create,' 'design,' 'carve.'</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary words from the previous lesson, focusing on syllable stress (e.g., 'sculpture' /ˈskʌlp.tʃər/, 'design' /dɪˈzaɪn/) and the accurate articulation of inflectional endings (-ed in 'created,' 'painted,' 'carved,' and -ing in 'making,' 'painting'). The teacher then models how to quickly scan a short text to identify main topics by looking for keywords and visual cues, demonstrating the reading strategy for the day.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If an artist 'sculpts' something, are they painting a picture or creating a 3D object?" (Expected answer: "Creating a 3D object.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When a building has a 'unique' design, does it look like all other buildings, or is it special?" (Expected answer: "It is special.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Time: 8-10 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Arts and Crafts".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students receive a short, simple informational text (e.g., a blog post or an excerpt from a local cultural magazine) titled "The Colorful Creations of a Mola Artist" or "Frank Gehry's Bold Design: The Biomuseo." The text describes the life and work of a Panamanian mola artist or the architectural details of the Biomuseo, incorporating grammar such as Present perfect ("This artist has created beautiful molas"), Present and past simple to describe achievements, and vocabulary like 'mola,' 'artist,' 'creative,' 'colorful,' 'handmade,' 'design,' 'building,' 'display,' 'exhibition.' It also mentions simple steps if describing mola creation (e.g., "First, you prepare the fabrics...").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Analyze simple informational texts related to arts and crafts, identifying key themes and viewpoints.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions:</b> The teacher asks, "What is the main idea of this text? Is it about food or about art?" and "Who is this text about?" Students read the text silently and then share their answers.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Statements:</b> Students read a series of True/False statements related to specific details in the text (e.g., "The artist has created sculptures." "The Biomuseo is a black and white building."). They mark T or F next to each statement.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match descriptions to vocabulary words from the text (e.g., "A place where art is shown" -> 'gallery' / 'exhibition'; "Someone who makes art" -> 'artist').</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Time: 10-12 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading, using target language in controlled activities.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading comprehension focused on identifying key information and practicing target grammar (Present perfect, Present/Past simple, Imperatives) and vocabulary (artist, craft, mola, design, create, colorful, unique, skillfully). Pronunciation of -ed and -ing endings will be reinforced.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – Cloze Reading:</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with a modified version of the text from Stage 2, containing blanks for target vocabulary words (e.g., 'artist,' 'mola,' 'colorful,' 'created,' 'designed') or verb tenses (-ed, -ing). Students fill in the blanks using a provided word bank, paying attention to context and grammatical form. For phonemic awareness, the teacher highlights verbs with -ed/-ing endings for students to focus on their pronunciation after filling in.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice – Sentence Reconstruction:</b> Students receive sentences from the original text but with certain words underlined. In pairs, they replace the underlined words with synonyms or related vocabulary from the lesson, ensuring the sentence meaning remains similar. For example, "The artist has <u>made</u> beautiful sculptures" becomes "The artist has <u>created</u> beautiful sculptures." They also practice reading these new sentences aloud, emphasizing correct syllable stress and inflectional endings.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Reading & Peer Check:</b> Students work in pairs. Each student receives a short, unique descriptive paragraph (2-3 sentences) about a different aspect of a local craft (e.g., how to make a basket, or the design of a tagua carving). They read their paragraph silently and then summarize it orally to their partner, using phrases like "First, you prepare..." (imperatives) or "This artist has crafted..." (present perfect). Their partner listens and checks for understanding by asking one follow-up question.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Time: 12-15 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Arts and Crafts".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group receives a different informational text (or a different section of a longer text) about a specific local artist, a craft (like basket weaving or tagua carving), or a famous Panamanian building known for its design (e.g., the Biomuseo). Examples of text topics: "The History of the Emberá Basket," "The Tagua Carvers of Coclé," "The Architecture of Old Panama City."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Group Visual Presentation Summary.</b> Each group reads their assigned text collaboratively, identifying key information about the artist, craft, or building. Their task is to create a simple visual presentation (a large drawing on a poster paper or a small model if materials are available) that summarizes the main points of their text. They must incorporate at least 5-7 target vocabulary words and use the present perfect to describe achievements (e.g., "This artist has crafted intricate designs") and/or imperatives to describe creation steps (e.g., "First, you prepare the fibers"). They should also include phrases describing creations (e.g., "The building is a two-story structure, with a rectangular design and white stone facades."). Each group prepares to briefly present their visual summary to the class. The focus is on analyzing the text and then visually representing the information, linking back to the reading objective.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Time: 10-12 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group work and presentation, focusing on reading comprehension evidenced by the visual summary and the use of target language. Peer assessment during presentations where students give constructive feedback on clarity and content.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group presents their visual summary (drawing/model) and briefly explains the key information from their text to the class (1-2 minutes per group). Other students listen and can ask one question.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of Summary:</b> How well does the visual and oral presentation reflect the main ideas of the text?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Inclusion and appropriate use of at least 5-7 words (artist, craft, mola, design, create, colorful, unique, etc.).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar Application:</b> Correct use of present perfect, present, and past simple to describe achievements, and imperatives when relevant.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Phonemic Awareness:</b> Intelligible pronunciation of words, especially those with -ed and -ing endings.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Evidence of teamwork in creating the visual summary.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences before leaving:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new fact I learned about local art/artists today is..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Reading about [topic of text] helped me understand [vocabulary word] better."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Time: 5-7 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Review of the lesson and connection to future learning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do you feel now about analyzing a text and identifying key information about artists or crafts?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of the reading activities did you find most helpful for understanding the target vocabulary and grammar?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The reading comprehension tasks successfully engaged students with the theme and target vocabulary. The visual summary activity helped students consolidate their understanding of the texts. Some students may need additional support with identifying main ideas in longer sentences. I will plan to provide more scaffolded reading strategies and graphic organizers in future reading lessons. Explicit phonemic awareness practice for -ed and -ing endings should be continued.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction), students will use the information and ideas gathered from reading these texts to discuss their favorite local artists and crafts, express opinions, and share facts about different art forms and buildings they have learned about today. They will practice describing creations and achievements of artists in more interactive communicative tasks.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Time: 2-3 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>